<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John of Montfort</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1283">1283</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John of Montfort was lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> in the crusader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a> from 1270, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-of-montfort">Philip of Montfort</a>, who was murdered by an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-assassins">Assassin</a>. He kept the city's trade alive through truces with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultans. After his death his heirs held Tyre until the Mamluks took it without resistance in 1291.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>England had sided with <b>John of Montfort</b> in the Breton Civil War, France with Charles of Blois.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-30">30 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>His son <b>John of Montfort</b> and later heirs held it until 1291, when, after the fall of Acre, the Mamluks took Tyre without resistance.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-tyre">Lordship of Tyre</a></li></ul></section>
